//! lotus/top_tail.rye — ALES98: the top-and-tail trimmer — segment the clip through ALES95, then cut ONLY the leading and trailing silence, keeping every internal pause.
//! ALES96 removes EVERY silent run and closes ranks; ALES97 cuts AT every silence and keeps each region apart. Between those two lives the single most-used silence
//! gesture in every recorder, DAW, and voice-memo app: TOP AND TAIL — trim only the dead air at the START and the END, and leave every pause INSIDE the recording
//! exactly where the speaker left it. A podcaster clears the throat, waits, speaks two sentences with a natural breath between them, then reaches for the button —
//! top-and-tail drops the ums at the ends and keeps the breath, because the breath is the performance and the dead air is not.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: segment [0, clip.len) through ALES95, find the FIRST non-silent run and the LAST non-silent run, then cut the trailing silence (from the
//! last non-silent run's end to the clip's end) and the leading silence (from the clip's start to the first non-silent run's start) through ALES2's own cut. The tail
//! is cut FIRST, so the leading run's start index stays valid — a suffix cut shifts nothing before it; then the head is cut. Every silent run BETWEEN the first and
//! last non-silent runs is left untouched.
//!
//! The crux — two laws, each tying this rung to a proven predecessor so it can never drift. THE SPAN LAW: the top-and-tailed clip equals, byte for byte, the original's
//! samples over [first_nonsilent.start, last_nonsilent.start + last_nonsilent.count) — the original narrowed to exactly the span from its first non-silent sample to its
//! last, internal silence kept. THE BRACKET LAW: the result equals the original bracketed by ALES97's outer takes (original[takes[0].start, takes[last].end)) byte for
//! byte, since the coalesce preserves the outer non-silent boundaries; and relative to ALES96, trim_silence.len <= top_and_tail.len <= original.len — top-and-tail keeps
//! every sample the full trimmer keeps PLUS the internal silences between them, so the two lengths are equal exactly when the clip carries no interior silence.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES98.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-234106_fill-ales98-lotus-top-tail-trimmer.md
//! Stands on: lotus/segment.rye (ALES95) — the voice-activity segmenter, reused by name so it keeps exactly the span the segmenter's outer non-silent runs name;
//! lotus/trim_silence.rye (ALES96) — the full silence stripper, the length-bracket twin; lotus/split_silence.rye (ALES97) — the auto-splitter, the outer-boundary twin;
//! lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and cut; lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94) — the .silent verdict.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. A bounded in-process buffer of i16 PCM on one bench, siloed to lotus/. This rung WRITES — it edits the clip — yet only
//! by removing whole classified-silent frames at the two ends through ALES2's own cut; it fabricates no sample and reorders nothing, and it never touches a sample
//! between the first and last non-silent runs. NOT a transcript, not a real adaptive VAD with lookahead; those stay later rungs. No real sample rate, no network, no
//! keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/top_tail.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const voiced = @import("voiced.rye");
const segment = @import("segment.rye");
const trim_silence = @import("trim_silence.rye");
const split_silence = @import("split_silence.rye");

pub const top_tail_version = "20260814.234106";

/// The top-and-tail trimmer's faults are the segmenter's OWN faults (ALES95's SegmentError) reused by name for the analysis and the editor's OWN faults (ALES2's
/// EditError from cut) reused by name for the two end cuts. The trimmer invents none of its own — an empty clip refuses BadFrame through the segmenter (a zero span
/// cannot carry a legal frame), so a keeper reads exactly one error vocabulary across the analysis and the edit.
pub const TopTailError = segment.SegmentError || timeline.EditError;

/// Trim ONLY the leading and trailing silence from `clip`, leaving every internal pause exactly where it lies. Segments the whole clip `[0, clip.len)` into frames of
/// `frame_len` samples through ALES95, finds the first and last `.silent`-negated (voiced or unvoiced) runs, then cuts the trailing silence and the leading silence
/// through ALES2's `cut` — tail first so the leading index stays valid. Validates the whole span, band, floor, and frame length through ALES95's own checks BEFORE any
/// cut; on refusal the clip is untouched. An empty clip refuses `BadFrame`. An all-silent clip trims to empty (no non-silent run exists); a no-silent clip is unchanged.
pub fn top_and_tail(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    frame_len: u32,
    t_low: i32,
    t_high: i32,
    silence_floor: u32,
    voice_split: u32,
) TopTailError!void {
    const original_len: u32 = clip.len;

    // Segment the whole clip once — the boundaries are exactly the segmenter's, which are exactly the classifier's, so this rung can never keep a span the segmenter
    // did not name. All of ALES95's validation (precheck for band/range, the floor ceiling, the dividing frame length, the empty-clip BadFrame) runs here before a cut.
    var segs = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(clip, &segs, 0, original_len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, silence_floor, voice_split);

    // invariant: segmenting a non-empty clip yields at least one run that tiles it — the segmenter's own postcondition, relied on here.
    assert(segs.len >= 1 and segs.len <= segment.max_segments);

    // Find the FIRST and LAST non-silent runs. `found` stays false only when every run is silent (an all-silence clip), in which case the whole clip is dead air.
    var found: bool = false;
    var first_ns: u32 = 0;
    var last_ns: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < segs.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (segs.items[i].verdict != .silent) {
            if (!found) {
                first_ns = i;
                found = true;
            }
            last_ns = i;
        }
    }

    if (!found) {
        // ALL-SILENT: no living span to keep — the whole clip is leading-and-trailing silence, so top-and-tail removes it all.
        // invariant: cutting the whole span leaves the clip empty; the span is legal since it is exactly the clip.
        try timeline.cut(clip, 0, original_len);
        assert(clip.len == 0);
        return;
    }

    // The kept span runs from the first non-silent sample to the last non-silent sample, internal silence included.
    const lead_count: u32 = segs.items[first_ns].start; // samples before the first non-silent run — the leading silence
    const kept_end: u32 = segs.items[last_ns].start + segs.items[last_ns].count; // one past the last non-silent sample
    // invariant: the kept span sits inside the clip and is non-empty (a non-silent run exists), so lead <= kept_end <= original.
    assert(lead_count <= kept_end and kept_end <= original_len);

    // Cut the TRAILING silence first (from kept_end to the clip's end). A suffix cut shifts nothing before it, so the leading index stays valid for the next cut.
    const tail_count: u32 = original_len - kept_end;
    if (tail_count > 0) {
        // invariant: the tail span lies wholly at the clip's end.
        assert(kept_end + tail_count == clip.len);
        try timeline.cut(clip, kept_end, tail_count);
    }

    // Cut the LEADING silence (from the clip's start, `lead_count` samples). Everything after it — the kept span — shifts down to the front, internal pauses intact.
    if (lead_count > 0) {
        // invariant: the leading span still lies at the front — the tail cut touched only samples after it.
        assert(lead_count <= clip.len);
        try timeline.cut(clip, 0, lead_count);
    }

    // invariant: the trimmed clip holds exactly the living span — its length is the last non-silent end minus the first non-silent start.
    assert(clip.len == kept_end - lead_count);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE SPAN LAW — the top-and-tailed clip equals the original over [first_ns.start, last_ns.end) byte for byte; THE BRACKET LAW — it equals the original
//     bracketed by ALES97's outer takes, and trim_silence.len <= top_and_tail.len <= original.len (equal to the trim only when no interior silence); an all-silent clip
//     trims to empty; a no-silent clip is unchanged; the INTERNAL pause is KEPT (the top-tail is strictly longer than the full trim on a clip with an interior gap); an
//     empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each refuse by name, the clip untouched -------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

/// The hand-built expectation the trimmer is checked against in the span law — segment the ORIGINAL, find the first and last non-silent runs, and copy the original's
/// samples over [first.start, last.end) into `out`. Kept independent of top_and_tail's own loop so the crux compares two paths, not one against itself. An all-silent
/// clip leaves `out` empty.
fn by_hand(out: *timeline.Clip, src: *const timeline.Clip, frame_len: u32, t_low: i32, t_high: i32, floor: u32, split: u32) !void {
    var segs = segment.Segments{};
    try segment.segment(src, &segs, 0, src.len, frame_len, t_low, t_high, floor, split);
    var found: bool = false;
    var first_ns: u32 = 0;
    var last_ns: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < segs.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (segs.items[i].verdict != .silent) {
            if (!found) {
                first_ns = i;
                found = true;
            }
            last_ns = i;
        }
    }
    if (!found) return; // all silence — nothing kept
    const start = segs.items[first_ns].start;
    const end = segs.items[last_ns].start + segs.items[last_ns].count;
    const samples = src.samples();
    try timeline.splice(out, 0, samples[start..end]);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const band_low: i32 = -1000;
    const band_high: i32 = 1000;

    // A signal with a quiet head, a loud voiced body, a quiet interior gap, a loud noisy run, and a quiet tail — the classic dead-air-at-the-ends recording plus an
    // interior gap. Frames of 4 samples. Quiet runs classify silent; the big-positive/big-negative body voiced; the alternating run unvoiced.
    const mixed = [_]i16{
        40,    -40,   40,    -40, // silent (quiet head)
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced
        20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced
        30,    -30,   30,    -30, // silent (interior gap)
        20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, // unvoiced (loud noisy)
        50,    -50,   50,    -50, // silent (quiet tail)
    };

    // 1. THE SPAN LAW — over many frame lengths, floors, and splits, top_and_tail equals the hand-built span from the first non-silent sample to the last, read from
    //    the original, internal silence kept.
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 }; // each divides 24
        const floors = [_]u32{ 100, 500, 1000, 5000 }; // all above the quiet ~40 rms, below the loud
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4 };
        var li: u32 = 0;
        while (li < flens.len) : (li += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var got = timeline.Clip{};
                    try load_clip(&got, &mixed);
                    var src = timeline.Clip{};
                    try load_clip(&src, &mixed);
                    var want = timeline.Clip{};
                    try top_and_tail(&got, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    try by_hand(&want, &src, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                    if (!eq(got.samples(), want.samples())) return error.NotTheLivingSpan;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 2. THE BRACKET LAW — top_and_tail equals the original bracketed by ALES97's outer takes, byte for byte, and its length sits between ALES96's full trim and the
    //    original (equal to the trim only when there is no interior silence).
    {
        const flens = [_]u32{ 1, 2, 4, 6, 12 };
        const floors = [_]u32{ 500, 1000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 2 };
        var li: u32 = 0;
        while (li < flens.len) : (li += 1) {
            var fi: u32 = 0;
            while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
                var si: u32 = 0;
                while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                    var src = timeline.Clip{};
                    try load_clip(&src, &mixed);

                    // The split's outer takes name the bracket.
                    var takes = split_silence.Takes{};
                    try split_silence.split_on_silence(&src, &takes, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);

                    var got = timeline.Clip{};
                    try load_clip(&got, &mixed);
                    try top_and_tail(&got, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);

                    // Full trim through ALES96 — the length lower bound.
                    var trimmed = timeline.Clip{};
                    try load_clip(&trimmed, &mixed);
                    try trim_silence.trim_silence(&trimmed, flens[li], band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);

                    if (takes.len == 0) {
                        // all silence — top-tail empty, trim empty
                        if (got.len != 0 or trimmed.len != 0) return error.AllSilentBracket;
                    } else {
                        const bracket_start = takes.items[0].start;
                        const bracket_end = takes.items[takes.len - 1].start + takes.items[takes.len - 1].count;
                        const bracket = mixed[bracket_start..bracket_end];
                        if (!eq(got.samples(), bracket)) return error.NotBracketedBySplit;
                        // trim_silence.len <= top_and_tail.len <= original.len
                        if (!(trimmed.len <= got.len and got.len <= mixed.len)) return error.LengthBracketBroken;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }

    // 3. THE INTERNAL PAUSE IS KEPT — on the mixed clip (frame 4, floor 500) the interior silent gap survives, so top-and-tail is STRICTLY longer than the full trim.
    //    top-tail keeps [4, 20) = 16 samples (body + gap + noisy); trim keeps only the 12 non-silent samples.
    {
        var top = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&top, &mixed);
        try top_and_tail(&top, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        const want = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // the two voiced frames
            30,    -30,   30,    -30, // the interior gap — KEPT, unlike ALES96
            20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, // the unvoiced frame
        };
        if (!eq(top.samples(), &want)) return error.InteriorGapNotKept;
        if (top.len != 16) return error.TopTailWrongLength;

        var trimmed = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&trimmed, &mixed);
        try trim_silence.trim_silence(&trimmed, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (!(trimmed.len < top.len)) return error.NotStrictlyLonger; // 12 < 16
    }

    // 4. AN ALL-SILENT CLIP trims to empty (no living span).
    {
        const quiet = [_]i16{ 40, -40, 40, -40, 30, -30, 30, -30 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &quiet);
        try top_and_tail(&clip, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (clip.len != 0) return error.AllSilentNotEmpty;
    }

    // 5. A NO-SILENT CLIP is unchanged — no dead air at either end to cut.
    {
        const loud = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &loud);
        try top_and_tail(&clip, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &loud)) return error.NoSilentChanged;
    }

    // 6. ONLY-LEADING and ONLY-TRAILING — a head-silent, tail-loud clip loses just its head; a head-loud, tail-silent clip loses just its tail.
    {
        const head_silent = [_]i16{
            40,    -40,   40,    -40, // silent head
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced tail
        };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &head_silent);
        try top_and_tail(&clip, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        const want_head = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &want_head)) return error.HeadNotTrimmed;

        const tail_silent = [_]i16{
            20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, // voiced head
            50,    -50,   50,    -50, // silent tail
        };
        var clip2 = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip2, &tail_silent);
        try top_and_tail(&clip2, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        const want_tail = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000 };
        if (!eq(clip2.samples(), &want_tail)) return error.TailNotTrimmed;
    }

    // 7a. AN EMPTY CLIP refuses BadFrame (nothing to trim), untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        if (top_and_tail(&clip, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 2)) |_| return error.EmptySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongEmpty;
        if (clip.len != 0) return error.EmptyMutated;
    }

    // 7b. A RAGGED FRAME LENGTH — one that does not divide the clip — refuses BadFrame, the clip untouched.
    {
        const five = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &five);
        if (top_and_tail(&clip, 2, band_low, band_high, 500, 2)) |_| return error.RaggedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFrame) return error.WrongRagged;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &five)) return error.RaggedMutated;
    }

    // 7c. AN INVERTED BAND refuses BadThreshold (through ALES95's precheck), the clip untouched.
    {
        const four = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &four);
        if (top_and_tail(&clip, 2, 1000, -1000, 500, 2)) |_| return error.InvertedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongInverted;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &four)) return error.InvertedMutated;
    }

    // 7d. AN ILLEGAL FLOOR — above the peak ceiling — refuses BadFloor, the clip untouched.
    {
        const four = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &four);
        if (top_and_tail(&clip, 2, band_low, band_high, 32769, 2)) |_| return error.BadFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFloor) return error.WrongBadFloor;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &four)) return error.BadFloorMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-top-tail: the TOP-AND-TAIL TRIMMER — segment the clip through ALES95, then cut ONLY the leading and trailing silence, keeping every internal " ++
        "pause. The rule: segment [0, clip.len) into frames of frame_len samples, find the FIRST and LAST non-silent runs, then cut the trailing silence (tail " ++
        "first, so the leading index stays valid) and the leading silence through ALES2's cut, leaving every silent run BETWEEN them untouched. The single most-used " ++
        "silence gesture in every recorder and DAW — drop the dead air at the ends, keep the breath inside. The crux is two laws. THE SPAN LAW: the trimmed clip " ++
        "equals the original over [first_nonsilent.start, last_nonsilent.end) byte for byte — the original narrowed to its living span, internal silence kept, across " ++
        "many frame lengths, floors, and splits. THE BRACKET LAW: it equals the original bracketed by ALES97's outer takes byte for byte, and trim_silence.len <= " ++
        "top_and_tail.len <= original.len — top-tail keeps every sample ALES96's full trimmer keeps PLUS the internal silences, equal only when there is no interior " ++
        "gap. The interior pause is KEPT (strictly longer than the full trim on a gapped clip); an all-silent clip trims to empty; a no-silent clip is unchanged; a " ++
        "head-silent clip loses only its head, a tail-silent clip only its tail; an empty clip, a ragged frame length, an inverted band, and an illegal floor each " ++
        "refuse by name — BadFrame, BadFrame, BadThreshold, BadFloor — the clip untouched. Purely local — it edits only by removing whole classified-silent frames " ++
        "at the two ends through cut, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing; NOT a transcript or a real adaptive VAD; no real sample rate, no network, no " ++
        "keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
